          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been tired and light-headed for months, my heart races on stairs, and my periods are heavy. Is that urgent, what could iron deficiency do to the blood, and which ICD-10 chapter covers anemia?",
          "answer": "It is important to note that your symptoms—including fatigue, light-headedness, a racing heart while exerting yourself, and heavy periods—might warrant urgent medical attention. Please consult a healthcare provider promptly.\n\nIron deficiency can lead to anemia, which is characterized by fatigue, reduced exercise tolerance, breathlessness, palpitations, headache, and dizziness. The body compensates for iron deficiency initially, but when it fails, these symptoms can become pronounced (source: \"Iron-deficiency anemia\").\n\nRegarding the ICD-10 classification, anemia is captured in Chapter 3, which covers diseases of the blood and blood-forming organs. Codes for anemia can be found in the range D50 to D89 (source: \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 3: blood, blood-forming organs and the immune mechanism\").\n\nWhat I could not cover is the specific urgency of your symptoms without a clinical assessment. Please seek immediate care for a proper evaluation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
